  "factCheck": {
    "status": "verified",
    "by": "HeyMariner daily desk",
    "checkedAt": "2026-11-17",
    "notes": "Barrass simplified estimate: maximum squat (m) = Cb x V^2 / 100 in open water and 2 x Cb x V^2 / 100 (= /50) in confined water, V in knots relative to the water (Oways, Maritime Page). Squat varies with the square of speed; full-form ships (high Cb, e.g. tankers, bulk carriers) have maximum squat at the bow (The Pilot). Arithmetic checked: 0.8x100/100 = 0.80; 0.8x100/50 = 1.60; 0.8x49/50 = 0.784. Vessel data is illustrative. It is an empirical estimate: the ship's own squat tables / pilot card and company UKC policy take precedence."
  },
